4. Critical Requirement: Needle3 Integration
Official model repository
Use the following Hugging Face repository as the canonical model source:
https://huggingface.co/Cactus-Compute/needle3
The official project is also available at:
https://github.com/cactus-compute/needle
Model documentation:
https://www.cactuscompute.com/needle
Default model behavior
Needle3 must be the first-party default lightweight local model presented during onboarding and the default local model selection after a successful installation.
Use the display name:
Needle3 — Learnova Lightweight AI
Show the model's actual installed status, download size, supported capabilities, and device compatibility.
Do not falsely report that Needle3 is installed simply because its catalog entry is visible.
If the model cannot be loaded on a device, explain the reason and provide compatible alternatives.
Understand the model's intended capabilities
Needle3 is a compact model designed particularly for:
•	Local tool selection and function calling.
•	Structured data extraction.
•	Classification-like tasks.
•	Text embeddings and similarity-based operations.
•	Lightweight on-device AI workflows.
Do not assume that it offers the same general conversation, extended reasoning, or academic explanation quality as a larger language model.
Before integration, evaluate its suitability for:
•	Short educational questions.
•	Simple explanations.
•	Structured extraction from imported text.
•	Local retrieval support.
•	Quiz question generation.
•	Application tool routing.
•	Structured outputs and confidence-based decisions.
Run representative educational tests. Record failures and unsupported tasks.
For complex tutoring, long-form explanations, or tasks that exceed Needle3's capabilities, let the user choose another compatible model or an online provider.
Do not silently send a user's question or document to a cloud provider when the local model fails.
Do not assume runtime compatibility
Needle3 uses its own .cact model format and associated inference engine.
Do not attempt to load needle3.cact directly with a generic GGUF/llama.cpp runtime unless the model publisher explicitly provides a supported conversion and documents that workflow.
Investigate the publisher's actual Android integration path, native libraries, headers, model-loading API, supported ABIs, and licensing requirements.
The Hugging Face repository includes Android-related engine artifacts. Determine whether the current artifacts provide everything needed for a reliable Android application integration.
Specifically:
1.	Inspect the official model repository and current documentation.
2.	Identify the appropriate Android engine and model files.
3.	Determine whether the documented native C API can be integrated into Kotlin through JNI or an appropriate native bridge.
4.	Confirm supported Android ABIs, minimum OS requirements, and native library dependencies.
5.	Verify how model loading, inference, streaming, cancellation, embeddings, tool calls, and cleanup work.
6.	Build a minimal native integration proof of concept.
7.	Test it on an actual Android device or emulator that satisfies the documented requirements.
8.	Document any missing Android integration steps.
If the available publisher artifacts do not provide a verified Android integration path, implement a clean runtime interface and a working integration investigation branch. Do not fabricate API functions, invent unsupported build commands, or claim successful inference.
In that situation, clearly report the blocker and continue implementing the rest of Learnova using a mockable runtime interface and, where practical, another verified local model runtime.
Model selection and download
Implement a dedicated model manager.
For Needle3, it must:
•	Retrieve model metadata from the official repository.
•	Pin a known model revision for reproducible downloads.
•	Resolve the correct model artifact and Android engine for the device.
•	Display download size before starting.
•	Check available storage.
•	Download with progress reporting.
•	Support cancellation and safe retry.
•	Resume downloads when supported by the source and transfer mechanism.
•	Verify artifact integrity using trusted published checksums where available.
•	Verify licenses and redistribution permissions.
•	Store the model and runtime files in appropriate application storage.
•	Validate the installation before making the model selectable.
•	Support deleting model files and recovering storage.
•	Provide understandable errors when files are missing or incompatible.
Do not download the entire repository when only a subset of files is required.
Do not hard-code fragile download URLs when repository metadata or a documented download mechanism can be used.
Do not treat the model's small file size as proof that its runtime memory requirements are equally small. Measure actual peak memory use, initialization time, and inference performance.
Additional models
Support a catalog of other compatible models from Hugging Face or other legitimate sources.
Each model entry must declare:
•	Model name and publisher.
•	Repository and pinned revision.
•	Model format.
•	Supported runtime.
•	Required runtime files.
•	License.
•	Download size.
•	Estimated memory requirements, when known.
•	Minimum supported device characteristics, when verified.
•	Supported tasks.
•	Known limitations.
•	Whether it works fully offline.
•	Whether it supports embeddings, structured outputs, tool calls, or multimodal input.
Do not present a model as Android-compatible simply because it is hosted on Hugging Face.
Never execute downloaded model files or native libraries without validating the source, integrity, and installation procedure.
