7. Local Model Runtime
Create a common interface so the UI does not depend directly on Needle3 or any specific model implementation.
Example conceptual operations:
•	Check compatibility.
•	Check installation status.
•	Download required assets.
•	Load model.
•	Initialize session.
•	Generate a response.
•	Stream supported output.
•	Cancel inference.
•	Generate embeddings, if supported.
•	Execute structured inference or tool selection, if supported.
•	Release model resources.
•	Report runtime errors and measured resource usage.
The actual interface must reflect the capabilities of the chosen runtime rather than assuming every runtime supports every operation.
For Needle3, use the verified publisher-supported engine and model format.
For additional models, allow other runtime adapters, including llama.cpp where the model format and Android integration are genuinely supported.
Never block the UI while loading a model or performing inference.
Avoid loading multiple large models simultaneously on low-memory devices unless a measured resource budget demonstrates that this is safe.
Provide graceful handling for:
•	Insufficient memory.
•	Unsupported ABI.
•	Missing model files.
•	Invalid model format.
•	Initialization failures.
•	Device thermal pressure.
•	Low storage.
•	Process interruption.
