9. Document Import and RAG
Support:
•	PDF.
•	DOCX.
•	PPTX.
•	TXT.
•	Markdown.
Use Android's document picker and process files locally by default.
Processing pipeline
1.	Import the file.
2.	Validate its type and size.
3.	Extract text and structural metadata.
4.	Preserve PDF page numbers, PowerPoint slide numbers, and Word headings where possible.
5.	Normalize extracted text.
6.	Split it into coherent chunks.
7.	Generate embeddings using a verified compatible embedding implementation.
8.	Store chunks and metadata locally.
9.	Retrieve relevant passages for a user question.
10.	Generate an answer using the selected AI mode.
11.	Display source references.
12.	Allow the user to inspect the source passage.
Use a modular retrieval implementation that supports:
•	Keyword search.
•	Vector similarity search.
•	Optional hybrid retrieval.
•	Metadata filtering.
•	Workspace isolation.
Do not assume Needle3 embeddings are interchangeable with embeddings from unrelated models. Test the embedding behavior and similarity quality before relying on it for semantic retrieval.
If Needle3's embedding functionality is suitable for the retrieval task, integrate it through the verified runtime. Otherwise, use a separate compatible embedding model or a keyword-based fallback.
Never claim that a document has been semantically indexed if only keyword indexing was performed.
Citation integrity
Every document-grounded answer must distinguish retrieved evidence from general model knowledge.
Citations must refer to actual retrieved passages.
Use:
•	PDF page numbers.
•	PPTX slide numbers.
•	DOCX headings or paragraph references.
Do not fabricate source references.
When relevant evidence cannot be retrieved, tell the user instead of presenting an unsupported answer as document-grounded.
Offline requirement
After documents have been imported and indexed, supported retrieval and local-model answering must work without an internet connection.
Cloud-only operations must be clearly marked.
